    why: "The objective is to match a distribution, not to reconstruct one particular target image for each noise vector.",
    related: ["p_g", "Distribution matching", "Sampling"],
  },
  latentVariable: {
    type: "Probability Concept",
    title: "Latent Variable / Noise Prior",
    body: "A hidden or unobserved random variable z. In GANs, we sample z from an easy prior p_z(z), such as a normal or uniform distribution, and transform it through the generator G.",
    why: "Different sampled z values provide the randomness needed to produce different outputs. This is not an encoder assigning a known image its latent code.",
    related: ["Generator", "Prior", "Latent interpolation"],
  },
  generator: {
    type: "GAN Component",
    title: "Generator (G)",
    body: "A differentiable function G(z; theta_g) that maps random noise into data space. Its induced sample distribution is p_g, even when p_g(x) cannot be evaluated directly.",
    why: "G is the model we ultimately want to use for generating samples; the discriminator exists to train it.",
    related: ["Implicit model", "Backpropagation", "Discriminator"],
  },
  discriminator: {
    type: "GAN Component",
    title: "Discriminator (D)",
    body: "A classifier D(x; theta_d) that outputs an estimate of how likely x is to have come from real data rather than the current generator, assuming equal real/fake sampling proportions.",
    why: "Unlike a fixed image-quality metric, D learns what currently separates real and generated samples.",
    related: ["Binary cross-entropy", "Optimal discriminator", "Density ratio"],
  },
  implicitModel: {
    type: "Modeling Distinction",
    title: "Implicit Generative Model",
    body: "A model defined by its sampling mechanism, e.g. z ~ p_z then x = G(z). A normalized or tractable density p_g(x) is not part of the interface.",
    why: "This is the crucial computational trade-off: GANs can sample directly but cannot usually report exact test likelihoods.",
    related: ["Explicit density", "Parzen window", "Generator"],
  },
  graphicalModel: {
