<PaperLayout
  title="Generative Adversarial Nets"
  eyebrow="Generative Models · GAN · 2014 / NeurIPS"
  authors="Ian J. Goodfellow · Jean Pouget-Abadie · Mehdi Mirza · Bing Xu · David Warde-Farley · Sherjil Ozair · Aaron Courville · Yoshua Bengio"
  arxivUrl="https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.2661"
  subtitle="How a learned critic became a training signal for generation: the earlier methods, the origin story, the full mathematical argument, and what the experiments actually established."
  sections={sections}
  annotations={annotations}
  initialAnnotation="generativeModel"
  compactTitle={true}
>
  <section id="overview" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>

    <div class="guide-box">
      <strong>Reading guide</strong>
      <p>
        Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) introduce a fundamentally different way to train generative models.
        Earlier approaches often relied on difficult probability calculations, approximate inference, or Markov-chain
        sampling to learn complex data distributions. GANs instead turn generative modeling into a competition between
        two neural networks: a <strong>generator</strong> that produces synthetic samples and a
        <strong>discriminator</strong> that learns to distinguish them from real data. Rather than explicitly
        computing the probability of each sample, the generator learns from the discriminator's feedback.
        The central insight is that this adversarial process can drive the generated distribution toward the real
        data distribution. The paper establishes the theoretical foundation for this idea and demonstrates that
        it can work in practice.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      The question is simple to ask: can a neural network learn what real images look like well enough to draw new
      ones? A <button class="anno" data-anno="generativeModel">generative model</button> is supposed to capture an
      unknown <button class="anno" data-anno="dataDistribution">data distribution</button>, not just assign labels to
      existing examples. In 2014, many powerful models could describe complex distributions in principle, but their
      training required difficult probability calculations, approximate inference, or costly sampling procedures.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>GAN's change of perspective:</strong> instead of scoring a generated image using an explicitly computed
      probability, train a second neural network to distinguish genuine observations from generated ones. Let that
      network improve whenever the generator improves. Then use its gradients to guide generation.
    </p>

    <div class="gan-core-flow" aria-label="The high-level GAN learning loop">
      <div class="gan-flow-node latent"><span class="gan-step-tag">INPUT</span><strong>Random noise z</strong><small>Sample an easy prior</small></div>
      <div class="gan-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <div class="gan-flow-node generator"><span class="gan-step-tag">LEARN</span><strong>Generator G</strong><small>Create synthetic x</small></div>
      <div class="gan-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <div class="gan-flow-node discriminator"><span class="gan-step-tag">COMPARE</span><strong>Discriminator D</strong><small>Real vs generated</small></div>
      <div class="gan-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <div class="gan-flow-node feedback"><span class="gan-step-tag">UPDATE</span><strong>Gradients</strong><small>Improve both networks</small></div>
    </div>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">The paper in one sentence</span>
      <p>
        A generator learns to sample from the real data distribution by competing with a discriminator that
        continuously learns to identify the generator's mistakes; under ideal assumptions, the original game
        minimizes the Jensen–Shannon divergence between their distributions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      The striking mathematical result is not merely that generated images can look convincing. Under an optimal
      discriminator, the original adversarial objective reduces to a precise discrepancy between probability
      distributions:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          C(G)=-\log 4+
          2\cdot\mathrm{JSD}(p_{\mathrm{data}}\|p_g)
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      That identity explains <em>why the ideal training game has the correct target</em>. It does not guarantee that
      practical neural-network optimization will always reach it. Keeping that distinction clear is essential to
      understanding both the achievement and the limitations of this paper.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="landscape" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Before GANs: what earlier generative models tried</h2>

    <p>
      The underlying task predates this paper. Given a dataset of images, speech, or other observations, we would
      like to estimate the process that generates them. The distinction between <strong>describing a probability</strong>
      and <strong>drawing a sample</strong> becomes crucial here. Even when a model has an elegant probabilistic
      definition, computing its likelihood, learning its parameters, or obtaining samples may be difficult.
    </p>

    <h3>1. Likelihood and the hidden-variable problem</h3>
    <p>
      A natural starting point is <button class="anno" data-anno="mle">maximum likelihood estimation</button>:
      give high probability to observed training examples. For model parameters <code>θ</code>, a standard objective is:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \max_{\theta}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\log p_\theta(x_i)
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      Unfortunately, high-dimensional <button class="anno" data-anno="graphicalModel">probabilistic graphical models</button>
      often contain hidden causes such as pose, expression, or lighting. Marginalizing a latent variable can require
      <code>p(x) = ∫ p(z) p(x | z) dz</code>. The integral, the reverse inference <code>p(z | x)</code>, or both can be
      difficult even before we consider optimization.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Energy-based models and expensive normalization</h3>
    <p>
      Models such as the <button class="anno" data-anno="rbm">Restricted Boltzmann Machine (RBM)</button> and
      <button class="anno" data-anno="dbm">Deep Boltzmann Machine (DBM)</button> assign an energy to possible
      visible/hidden configurations. Low-energy configurations are relatively probable, but a normalized density
      also needs the <button class="anno" data-anno="partitionFunction">partition function</button>:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{gathered}
            p_\theta(x,z)=
            \frac{\exp(-E_\theta(x,z))}{Z_\theta}\\[8pt]
            Z_\theta=\sum_{x,z}\exp(-E_\theta(x,z))
          \end{gathered}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      The sum spans an enormous state space; for continuous variables, analogous integrals appear. Researchers often
      used <button class="anno" data-anno="mcmc">Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC)</button> to approximate gradients
      or draw samples. But chains can suffer from poor <button class="anno" data-anno="mixing">mixing</button>: they
      may spend a long time near one mode before visiting another. The hybrid
      <button class="anno" data-anno="dbn">Deep Belief Network (DBN)</button> faces difficulties inherited from both
      directed and undirected modeling.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Other learning objectives were already emerging</h3>
    <p>
      GANs were <strong>not</strong> the first attempt to escape direct normalized-likelihood calculations. In
      <button class="anno" data-anno="scoreMatching">score matching</button>, the target involves derivatives of the
      log density. In <button class="anno" data-anno="nce">noise-contrastive estimation (NCE)</button>, an estimation
      problem becomes a classification problem: distinguish genuine data from samples of a <em>fixed</em> noise
      distribution. NCE is particularly interesting because it already connects discrimination and generative learning.
    </p>
    <p>
      Separately, a <button class="anno" data-anno="dae">denoising autoencoder (DAE)</button> learns by reconstructing
      clean examples from corrupted examples. A <button class="anno" data-anno="gsn">Generative Stochastic Network
      (GSN)</button> can learn a stochastic transition kernel, but still uses a Markov chain when sampling. Contemporary
      work on <button class="anno" data-anno="vae">variational autoencoding</button> and stochastic backpropagation
      offered yet another route through learned approximate inference.
    </p>

    <div class="results-table-wrap">
      <table class="results-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Earlier approach</th>
            <th>Basic idea</th>
            <th>Typical mathematical picture</th>
            <th>Why GAN took another route</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {priorMethods.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.name}</td>
              <td>{row.idea}</td>
              <td class="prior-method-formula">
                {row.math ? (
                  <MathFormula formula={row.math} />
                ) : (
                  <span>{row.mathLabel}</span>
                )}
              </td>
              <td>{row.obstacle}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">What should not be confused</span>
      <p>
        Earlier work had already studied classifiers, backpropagation, contrastive learning, and neural sampling.
        The novelty is not that GAN discovered any of those elements individually. It combined an <em>independent,
        continually updated discriminator</em> with a <em>sample-producing generator</em> and optimized them against
        each other as a game. Unlike classical NCE, GAN does not need to evaluate the generator's own density to
        construct the discriminator objective.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="origin" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Where the idea came from</h2>

    <p>
      The paper itself does not give a detailed personal origin story. That history comes from later interviews with
      Ian Goodfellow, especially his
      <a
        class="anno"
        href="https://www.deeplearning.ai/the-batch/ian-goodfellow-a-man-a-plan-a-gan"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >2020 DeepLearning.AI interview</a>
      and
      <a
        class="anno"
        href="https://www.lerandom.art/editorial/ian-goodfellow-on-inventing-gans"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >2025 Le Random interview</a>.
      Those are first-person retrospectives, not claims established in the 2014 paper.
    </p>

    <h3>1. A farewell gathering and an impractical plan</h3>
    <p>
      At a Montreal gathering in late May 2014, other researchers described a way to train a generator by matching
      increasingly complex image statistics: first average pixel brightness, then pairwise correlations, then products
      across triples of pixels. The idea is intuitive: if generated and real images match enough statistics, perhaps
      they will become similar. But enumerating higher-order features explodes combinatorially.
    </p>
    <div class="comparison-grid">
      <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Pair statistics</h3>

        <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
          <MathFormula
            formula={String.raw`
              \binom{1000}{2}=499{,}500
            `}
          />
        </div>

        <p>
          Already nearly half a million distinct pairs
          for just 1,000 pixels.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Triple statistics</h3>

        <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
          <MathFormula
            formula={String.raw`
              \binom{1000}{3}=166{,}167{,}000
            `}
          />
        </div>

        <p>
          Over 166 million unordered triples before
          considering the entire batch or richer measurements.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      In Goodfellow's telling, his colleagues initially asked for GPU-programming advice. His response was that the
      difficulty was algorithmic, not something that better memory management could solve. Why enumerate enormous
      numbers of features if a neural network could <em>learn which features matter</em>?
    </p>

    <h3>2. Replace hand-designed statistics with a learned critic</h3>
    <div class="gan-comparison" aria-label="From manually enumerated statistics to a learned discriminator">
      <div class="gan-comparison-panel old">
        <span class="gan-step-tag">OLD PROPOSAL</span>
        <strong>Enumerate measurements</strong>
        <p>Mean pixel brightness → pairwise correlations → all triplet products → ...</p>
        <small>Feature list grows explosively as order increases.</small>
      </div>
      <div class="gan-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <div class="gan-comparison-panel new">
        <span class="gan-step-tag">KEY INSIGHT</span>
        <strong>Learn the measurements</strong>
        <p>A neural classifier discovers the features that distinguish real and generated images.</p>
        <small>Its representation and decision rule adapt as the generator changes.</small>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      There was an additional insight. Goodfellow had earlier considered using a discriminator to judge synthetic
      speech, but a fixed judge could be exploited. A generator might merely learn the judge's blind spots. So the
      judge itself should keep learning. Now the two-network game is no longer a decorative metaphor: it is a direct
      answer to the problem of <em>who should specify the generator's training criterion</em>.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Why the idea could be tested so quickly</h3>
    <p>
      Goodfellow reused existing supervised-network code to build the discriminator and much of the generator. He
      recalled obtaining recognizable MNIST samples shortly after going home. The quick result was possible because
      the researchers already had years of generative-model experience, working neural-network code, and a small
      proof-of-concept dataset. The story should not be interpreted as a complete research breakthrough produced
      from nothing in one evening.
    </p>
    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">The research lesson from the story</span>
      <p>
        The decisive move was <strong>problem reformulation</strong>. Instead of asking how to compute a massive
        set of prescribed image statistics efficiently, ask whether a flexible, learned discriminator can discover
        differences and send useful gradients back to the generator. This idea connects naturally to the related
        work: earlier researchers already knew how to train powerful classifiers, and NCE already linked
        discrimination with density learning. GAN made the comparison distribution itself adaptive.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="insight" class="reading-section">
    <h2>The conceptual leap: learn the test, not just the generator</h2>
    <p>
      We can now connect history to methodology. Imagine we want to generate handwritten digits. A naive approach
      is to compare generated images with real images pixel by pixel. But there is <em>no unique correct target</em>
      for a random noise vector. The point is not to produce a particular digit; the point is to produce samples
      whose overall distribution resembles genuine handwriting.
    </p>
    <p>
      A fixed hand-designed score may miss subtle artifacts or reward a narrow family of samples. A learned
      <button class="anno" data-anno="discriminator">discriminator</button> can ask a changing question: what best
      separates today's generated samples from real ones? The
      <button class="anno" data-anno="generator">generator</button> then moves its samples in a direction that makes
      the discriminator more likely to classify them as real.
    </p>
    <div class="gan-three-column">
      <div class="gan-insight-card">
        <span>STEP A</span><strong>Sample</strong><p>Draw noise and real data independently; there is no paired ground-truth image for each noise vector.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gan-insight-card">
        <span>STEP B</span><strong>Discover the gap</strong><p>Train a classifier to identify the current statistical differences between the two populations.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gan-insight-card">
        <span>STEP C</span><strong>Move the distribution</strong><p>Send the classification gradient backward through the synthetic sample into generator parameters.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Notice the shift: we are no longer specifying an exhaustive list of features that all good images must satisfy.
      We are constructing a <strong>differentiable, learned evaluation criterion</strong>. And because the criterion
      adapts as G improves, it need not be a permanently fixed set of tests.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="architecture" class="reading-section">
    <h2>The two-network system</h2>

    <h3>1. Define the two functions</h3>
    <p>
      The original formulation uses two <button class="anno" data-anno="mlp">multilayer perceptrons (MLPs)</button>.
      Start with a <button class="anno" data-anno="latentVariable">latent noise variable</button> sampled from an
      easy prior <code>p<sub>z</sub>(z)</code>. The generator maps it to data space:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          z\sim p_z(z)
          \quad\longrightarrow\quad
          x_{\mathrm{fake}}=G(z;\theta_g)
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      The resulting samples follow a distribution <code>p<sub>g</sub></code>, defined implicitly by this mapping.
      The <button class="anno" data-anno="implicitModel">implicit-model</button> distinction matters: the network
      can sample from <code>p<sub>g</sub></code> without evaluating the probability density of a particular x.
    </p>
    <p>
      Meanwhile, the discriminator outputs a scalar estimate in [0,1]:
      <code>D(x; θ<sub>d</sub>)</code>. Higher values mean more likely to be genuine training data, not a particular
      image class. For a fake image, the same network is evaluated as <code>D(G(z))</code>.
    </p>

