  <section id="math" class="reading-section">
    <h2>The mathematics in four steps</h2>
    <p>
      Everything that follows is built around one chain of reasoning. First construct the two-player training game.
      Then, for a fixed generator, solve for the best possible discriminator. Next substitute that discriminator
      back into the game. Finally show that the resulting objective is minimized exactly when the two distributions
      are identical.
    </p>
    <div class="gan-four-steps" aria-label="Four mathematical steps of the GAN proof">
      <div><b>01</b><strong>Define the game</strong><small>Binary real/fake log-likelihood</small></div>
      <div><b>02</b><strong>Solve for D*</strong><small>Optimal classifier for fixed G</small></div>
      <div><b>03</b><strong>Substitute D*</strong><small>KL terms become JSD</small></div>
      <div><b>04</b><strong>Prove the optimum</strong><small>JSD = 0 iff distributions match</small></div>
    </div>

    <h3>Step 1 — Define the minimax game</h3>
    <p>
      The discriminator is simply a binary classifier: real samples get label 1; generated samples get label 0.
      If it predicts a probability d and the label is y, ordinary
      <button class="anno" data-anno="bce">binary cross-entropy</button> is:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \operatorname{BCE}(y,d)=-\left[y\log d+(1-y)\log(1-d)\right]
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      For a real x, substitute <code>y = 1</code>. The loss is <code>−log D(x)</code>.
      For a fake sample G(z), substitute <code>y = 0</code>. The loss is
      <code>−log(1−D(G(z)))</code>. Take the average over both sample sources; equivalently maximize the negative loss.
      In the paper's notation, this yields equation:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{aligned}
          \min_G\max_D\; &V(D,G)\\[6pt]
          V(D,G)={}&\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{\mathrm{data}}}\!\left[\log D(x)\right]\\
          &+\mathbb{E}_{z\sim p_z}\!\left[\log(1-D(G(z)))\right]
          \end{aligned}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      The symbol <button class="anno" data-anno="expectation">E[·]</button> denotes an expectation: an average
      over a probability distribution, not a paired-image reconstruction error. And
      <button class="anno" data-anno="minimax">minimax</button> means that D increases this value while G tries to
      decrease it. Real images do not have to correspond to the random z vectors used in the same minibatch.
    </p>
    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">A numerical example of the discriminator's objective</span>
      <p>
        Suppose D assigns <code>D(x<sub>real</sub>) = 0.9</code> and
        <code>D(G(z)) = 0.1</code>. The one-real/one-fake discriminator BCE sum is
        <code>−log(0.9) − log(0.9) ≈ 0.2107</code>. If it instead assigns 0.5 to both,
        the sum becomes <code>−log(0.5) − log(0.5) ≈ 1.3863</code>.
        Lower BCE corresponds to better discrimination. In the paper's maximization convention,
        the value function is the negative of these sums.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      The generator's original minimax part is only the fake-sample term:
      <code>min<sub>G</sub> E<sub>z</sub>[log(1−D(G(z)))]</code>. It can influence
      <code>D(G(z))</code> only by changing its generated samples. The real-image term is independent of G
      when D's parameters are held fixed.
    </p>

    <h3>Step 2 — Derive the optimal discriminator D*</h3>
    <p>
      Now fix G, so <code>p<sub>g</sub></code> is fixed. The key trick is to rewrite the generated-sample
      expectation as an expectation under the distribution induced by G:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{aligned}
          &\mathbb{E}_{z\sim p_z}[\log(1-D(G(z)))]\\[5pt]
          &\qquad=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_g}[\log(1-D(x))]
          \end{aligned}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      This identity is a change of the random variable being averaged. It does <em>not</em> mean that the training
      algorithm has to compute <code>p<sub>g</sub>(x)</code>. In the idealized density notation used in the proof,
      the value becomes the integral:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{aligned}
          V(D,G)=\int\Big[&p_{\mathrm{data}}(x)\log D(x)\\
          &+p_g(x)\log(1-D(x))\Big] \,dx
          \end{aligned}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      At any given x, let <code>a = p<sub>data</sub>(x)</code>,
      <code>b = p<sub>g</sub>(x)</code>, and <code>d = D(x)</code>. We need to maximize:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{aligned}
          f(d)&=a\log d+b\log(1-d)\\[5pt]
          f'(d)&=\frac{a}{d}-\frac{b}{1-d}\\[5pt]
          f'(d)=0&\;\Longrightarrow\;a(1-d)=bd\\[5pt]
          &\;\Longrightarrow\;d^*=\frac{a}{a+b}
          \end{aligned}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      For positive a and b, the second derivative is
      <code>f″(d) = −a/d² − b/(1−d)² &lt; 0</code>, confirming a maximum.
      If just one density is positive, the optimum lies at the appropriate boundary, 0 or 1. If both densities
      vanish, D's prediction does not affect the objective. The result is Proposition 1:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          D_G^*(x)=\frac{p_{\mathrm{data}}(x)}{p_{\mathrm{data}}(x)+p_g(x)}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      This is the <button class="anno" data-anno="optimalD">optimal discriminator</button> for a fixed generator,
      assuming the same relative frequency of real and fake examples. It is closely connected to a Bayes-optimal
      real/fake classifier: the denominator asks how much probability both sources put on x; the numerator asks
      how much comes from real data.
    </p>

    <h4>Worked example: the generator reverses the cat/dog frequencies</h4>
    <p>
      Consider a deliberately tiny <em>discrete</em> world containing only cats and dogs. In the real data, cats
      occur 80% of the time; in generated data, they occur only 20% of the time:
    </p>
    <div class="results-table-wrap">
      <table class="results-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Observation x</th><th>p<sub>data</sub>(x)</th><th>p<sub>g</sub>(x)</th><th>D*<sub>G</sub>(x)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Cat</td><td>0.8</td><td>0.2</td><td>0.8 / (0.8 + 0.2) = <strong>0.8</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dog</td><td>0.2</td><td>0.8</td><td>0.2 / (0.2 + 0.8) = <strong>0.2</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A cat is more likely to be real than generated under the current model, so D* outputs 0.8. A dog is more
      likely to be generated, so D* outputs 0.2. Importantly, these predictions are about <em>which source
      produced the observed kind of sample</em>, not a cat-versus-dog classifier.
    </p>
    <div class="gan-bars" aria-label="Real and generated probabilities for the toy cat and dog example">
      <div class="gan-bar-group">
        <strong>Cat</strong>
        <div class="gan-bar-row"><span>Real</span><div class="gan-bar-track"><i class="real" style="width:80%"></i></div><em>0.8</em></div>
        <div class="gan-bar-row"><span>Fake</span><div class="gan-bar-track"><i class="fake" style="width:20%"></i></div><em>0.2</em></div>
      </div>
      <div class="gan-bar-group">
        <strong>Dog</strong>
        <div class="gan-bar-row"><span>Real</span><div class="gan-bar-track"><i class="real" style="width:20%"></i></div><em>0.2</em></div>
        <div class="gan-bar-row"><span>Fake</span><div class="gan-bar-track"><i class="fake" style="width:80%"></i></div><em>0.8</em></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Step 3 — Substitute D* and uncover Jensen–Shannon divergence</h3>
    <p>
      Now ask what happens to the whole game if the discriminator always reaches its optimum for the current G.
      Define <code>C(G) = max<sub>D</sub> V(D,G) = V(D*<sub>G</sub>,G)</code>.
      Substituting Step 2 into equation in Step 1 gives equation:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{aligned}
          C(G)={}&\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{\mathrm{data}}}\!\left[
          \log\frac{p_{\mathrm{data}}(x)}{p_{\mathrm{data}}(x)+p_g(x)}\right]\\[8pt]
          &+\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_g}\!\left[
          \log\frac{p_g(x)}{p_{\mathrm{data}}(x)+p_g(x)}\right]
          \end{aligned}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      The notation means the probability densities are evaluated at x inside their respective expectations;
      the arguments are omitted only to keep the formula readable. This expression looks unfamiliar, so introduce
      the average or mixture distribution:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          M(x)=\frac{p_{\mathrm{data}}(x)+p_g(x)}{2}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      Because <code>p<sub>data</sub> + p<sub>g</sub> = 2M</code>, the two logarithms can be separated:
      <code>log(p / 2M) = log(p / M) − log 2</code>. Each expectation of the constant
      <code>−log 2</code> contributes one copy, so together they give <code>−2 log 2 = −log 4</code>.
      What remains is two <button class="anno" data-anno="kl">Kullback–Leibler divergences</button>:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{gathered}
          C(G)=-\log4+D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_{\mathrm{data}}\|M)+D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_g\|M)\\[10pt]
          D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P\|Q)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}\!\left[\log\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}\right]
          \end{gathered}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      Now introduce the
      <button class="anno" data-anno="jsd">Jensen–Shannon divergence (JSD)</button>:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{aligned}
          \operatorname{JSD}(P\|Q)&=\tfrac12 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P\|M)
          +\tfrac12 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(Q\|M)\\[6pt]
          M&=\tfrac12(P+Q)
          \end{aligned}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      Our two KL terms are exactly twice this quantity. The entire adversarial game, after choosing the optimal D,
      therefore becomes:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation gan-key-result">
      <span class="gan-equation-label">THE KEY IDENTITY</span>
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          C(G)=-\log4+2\operatorname{JSD}(p_{\mathrm{data}}\|p_g)
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      This is the paper's deepest mathematical payoff. An apparently heuristic two-network competition is, under
      the optimal-discriminator assumption, equivalent to minimizing a well-defined measure of discrepancy between
      <em>entire probability distributions</em>. No pixel-by-pixel target matching, explicit p<sub>g</sub>(x)
      computation, or fixed feature inventory is required in the actual training procedure.
    </p>

    <h4>Numerical check with the same cat/dog example</h4>
    <p>
      Let <code>P = (0.8, 0.2)</code> be the real distribution and
      <code>Q = (0.2, 0.8)</code> the generated one. The mixture is <code>M = (0.5, 0.5)</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{aligned}
          D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P\|M)
          &=0.8\log\frac{0.8}{0.5}+0.2\log\frac{0.2}{0.5}\\
          &\approx0.1927\\[8pt]
          D_{\mathrm{KL}}(Q\|M)&\approx0.1927\\
          \operatorname{JSD}(P\|Q)&\approx0.1927\\[8pt]
          C(G)&=-1.3863+2(0.1927)\\
          &\approx-1.0008
          \end{aligned}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      If the generator learns <code>Q = (0.8, 0.2)</code>, the mixture is now exactly P and Q,
      each KL term becomes zero, and <code>C(G)</code> drops to <code>−log 4 ≈ −1.3863</code>.
      The objective becomes <em>smaller</em> as the generated distribution approaches the real one.
    </p>

    <h3>Step 4 — Prove the global optimum</h3>
    <p>
      JSD is never negative, and it equals zero if and only if its two arguments are identical as probability
      distributions. Therefore:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation gan-key-result">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{gathered}
          \operatorname{JSD}(p_{\mathrm{data}}\|p_g)\ge0\\[9pt]
          \min_G C(G)=-\log4\\[9pt]
          C(G)=-\log4\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad p_g=p_{\mathrm{data}}
          \end{gathered}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      This is Theorem 1. When the distributions match, the optimal discriminator is a coin toss wherever the common
      distribution has probability mass:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          D_G^*(x)=\frac{p_{\mathrm{data}}(x)}{p_{\mathrm{data}}(x)+p_{\mathrm{data}}(x)}=\frac12
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      A D output of 0.5 here is <strong>not evidence of a poorly trained classifier</strong>. If the two sources have
      identical distributions and are presented with equal prior frequency, no classifier observing only x can
      distinguish them better than chance. The result concerns distribution equality, not literal pixel-by-pixel
      identity between generated and training examples.
    </p>

    <h4>What about convergence? The role of Proposition 2</h4>
    <p>
      Theorem 1 tells us where the ideal global optimum lies. It does not automatically prove that a particular
      learning algorithm reaches it. This paper adds a distribution-space argument: for fixed D, the game value is
      <em>affine</em> in p<sub>g</sub>, because only the term
      <code>E<sub>x~p<sub>g</sub></sub>[log(1−D(x))]</code> depends on that distribution.
      Taking the pointwise supremum over all discriminators yields a
      <button class="anno" data-anno="convexity">convex function in p<sub>g</sub></button>.
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{aligned}
          U(p_g,D)={}&\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{\mathrm{data}}}[\log D(x)]\\
          &+\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_g}[\log(1-D(x))]\\[8pt]
          C(p_g)&=\sup_D U(p_g,D)\\
          &\text{is convex in }p_g
          \end{aligned}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      At a maximizing discriminator, an appropriate derivative of U supplies a subgradient of C. Under the paper's
      assumptions—enough model capacity, D optimized for the current G, and sufficiently small improvements to the
      probability distribution—the distribution-level optimization converges to the unique optimum
      <code>p<sub>g</sub> = p<sub>data</sub></code>. This is Proposition 2, not a theorem that alternating
      neural-network SGD is guaranteed to work with arbitrary initializations.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="training" class="reading-section">
    <h2>How training actually works</h2>
    <p>
      The elegant derivation makes an unrealistic demand: maximize over all D before every generator update.
      With a finite dataset and a neural network, doing this exactly would be computationally prohibitive and
      could overfit the discriminator. The paper therefore proposes alternating minibatch updates.
    </p>

    <h3>1. Alternate, do not optimize both networks indiscriminately</h3>
    <div class="gan-training-cycle" aria-label="Alternating GAN training steps">
      <div class="gan-cycle-card discriminator">
        <span class="gan-step-tag">DISCRIMINATOR PHASE · REPEAT k TIMES</span>
        <strong>Freeze G → train D</strong>
        <p>Draw real x and random z, make G(z), and ascend the gradient of log D(x) + log(1−D(G(z))).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gan-cycle-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div>
      <div class="gan-cycle-card generator">
        <span class="gan-step-tag">GENERATOR PHASE · ONCE</span>
        <strong>Freeze D → train G</strong>
        <p>Draw new z, feed G(z) through D, and update G using the gradient of the generator loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gan-cycle-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↻</div>
      <div class="gan-cycle-caption">Repeat for many minibatches. The authors report <strong>k = 1</strong> in their experiments.</div>
    </div>

    <p>
      Let the <code>m</code> real samples be <code>x<sup>(1)</sup>, ..., x<sup>(m)</sup></code> and
      the m noise samples be <code>z<sup>(1)</sup>, ..., z<sup>(m)</sup></code>. The D phase uses gradient
      <em>ascent</em> on the minibatch log-likelihood:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{aligned}
          \nabla_{\theta_d}\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}\Bigl[
          &\log D(x^{(i)})\\
          &+\log\bigl(1-D(G(z^{(i)}))\bigr)
          \Bigr]
          \end{aligned}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      The original minimax G phase uses gradient <em>descent</em> on:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \nabla_{\theta_g}\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}
          \log\bigl(1-D(G(z^{(i)}))\bigr)
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      The signs matter: optimizing D makes its real/fake classification more accurate; optimizing G tries to
      undermine D's judgments on fake samples. The paper uses ordinary gradient-based updates with momentum.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Where does the generator's learning signal come from?</h3>
    <p>
      A subtle point: the discriminator is not merely reporting a score. To update G, we need
      <button class="anno" data-anno="backprop">backpropagation</button> through the entire chain
      <code>z → G(z) → D(G(z)) → loss</code>. By the chain rule:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \frac{\partial L_G}{\partial\theta_g}
          =\frac{\partial L_G}{\partial D}
          \cdot\frac{\partial D}{\partial G}
          \cdot\frac{\partial G}{\partial\theta_g}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      These are derivatives through intermediate computational values. When training G, D's weights are held
      fixed—but the derivative of D's output with respect to its <em>input image</em> is still required.
      Without that derivative, a numeric "real/fake" judgment would not say how to change the generator.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Why the paper changes the generator loss</h3>
    <p>
      The original generator minimizes <code>log(1−D(G(z)))</code>. At the beginning of training, poor generated
      images are easy to reject; D may output a fake-is-real probability very close to zero. In this regime,
      the original generator objective can suffer from
      <button class="anno" data-anno="gradientSaturation">weak or saturated gradients</button>.
    </p>
    <p>
      We can see the issue exactly for a sigmoid discriminator output. Let <code>d = σ(a)</code>, where a is its
      pre-sigmoid logit. By differentiation, the original minimax generator term has:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{aligned}
          L_{G,\mathrm{minimax}}&=\log\bigl(1-\sigma(a)\bigr)\\[6pt]
          \frac{\partial L_{G,\mathrm{minimax}}}{\partial a}
          &=-\sigma(a)=-d
          \end{aligned}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      If D is extremely confident that a generated sample is fake, say <code>d = 0.001</code>, this gradient at
      the logit is only <code>−0.001</code>. The paper therefore recommends that G instead maximize
      <code>log D(G(z))</code>, or equivalently minimize the
      <button class="anno" data-anno="nonSaturating">non-saturating loss</button>:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{aligned}
          L_{G,\mathrm{NS}}&=-\mathbb{E}_{z\sim p_z}
          [\log D(G(z))]\\[6pt]
          \frac{\partial[-\log\sigma(a)]}{\partial a}
          &=-[1-\sigma(a)]=-(1-d)
          \end{aligned}
        `}
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      At the same <code>d = 0.001</code>, its logit gradient is <code>−0.999</code> instead of
      <code>−0.001</code>. That is a much stronger output-layer signal during early training. It does not guarantee
      that gradients remain large all the way through D and G, but it avoids one major source of saturation.
    </p>
    <div class="results-table-wrap">
      <table class="results-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Generator objective</th><th>Minimized expression</th><th>Logit gradient</th><th>At D(G(z)) = 0.001</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Original minimax</td>
            <td><MathFormula formula={String.raw`\log(1-d)`} displayMode={false} /></td>
            <td><MathFormula formula={String.raw`-d`} displayMode={false} /></td>
            <td><MathFormula formula={String.raw`-0.001`} displayMode={false} /></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Non-saturating</td>
            <td><MathFormula formula={String.raw`-\log d`} displayMode={false} /></td>
            <td><MathFormula formula={String.raw`-(1-d)`} displayMode={false} /></td>
            <td><MathFormula formula={String.raw`-0.999`} displayMode={false} /></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">A crucial theoretical caveat</span>
      <p>
        The non-saturating generator objective has the same desired ideal fixed point, but it is <strong>not</strong>
        algebraically identical to the minimax generator objective during training. In particular, the JSD identity
        in the four-step proof is derived for the <em>original minimax game</em>, not for an arbitrary replacement
        generator loss.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>4. Read figure as changing distributions, not moving labeled examples</h3>
    <p>
      The paper's figure shows the real distribution as a black dotted curve, the generator distribution as a
      green curve, and the discriminator as a blue dashed curve. The sequence is: the distributions differ;
      D learns to distinguish them; G follows D's signal into regions more likely to count as real; and under
      idealized convergence the distributions match while D becomes one-half.
    </p>
    <div class="gan-distribution-grid">
      <div class="gan-distribution-panel">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 190 105" role="img" aria-label="Initially the real and generated distributions have different peaks">
          <path d="M8 89 H184" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <path d="M8 87 C40 87 52 17 82 17 S125 87 184 87" fill="none" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.3" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
          <path d="M8 87 C83 87 116 35 140 34 S166 82 184 87" fill="none" stroke="#33906b" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <path d="M8 43 C34 30 53 59 76 46 S120 56 143 46 S172 54 184 47" fill="none" stroke="#477fc1" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 3"/>
        </svg>
        <b>(a) Two different distributions</b>
        <p>Generated samples are concentrated in the wrong region.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gan-distribution-panel">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 190 105" role="img" aria-label="The discriminator learns a steeper separation between real and fake regions">
          <path d="M8 89 H184" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <path d="M8 87 C40 87 52 17 82 17 S125 87 184 87" fill="none" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.3" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
          <path d="M8 87 C83 87 116 35 140 34 S166 82 184 87" fill="none" stroke="#33906b" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <path d="M8 29 C55 29 82 38 98 54 S143 79 184 79" fill="none" stroke="#477fc1" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 3"/>
        </svg>
        <b>(b) Train D</b>
        <p>D assigns greater real probability to regions favored by the data.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gan-distribution-panel">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 190 105" role="img" aria-label="The generated distribution shifts toward the real distribution">
          <path d="M8 89 H184" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <path d="M8 87 C40 87 52 17 82 17 S125 87 184 87" fill="none" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.3" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
          <path d="M8 87 C46 87 58 25 94 24 S135 83 184 87" fill="none" stroke="#33906b" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <path d="M8 29 C55 29 82 38 98 54 S143 79 184 79" fill="none" stroke="#477fc1" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 3"/>
        </svg>
        <b>(c) Train G</b>
        <p>The generator shifts the distribution to exploit D's learned signal.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gan-distribution-panel">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 190 105" role="img" aria-label="The two distributions match and the discriminator equals one half">
          <path d="M8 89 H184" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <path d="M8 87 C40 87 52 17 82 17 S125 87 184 87" fill="none" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="3.2" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
          <path d="M8 87 C40 87 52 17 82 17 S125 87 184 87" fill="none" stroke="#33906b" stroke-width="2"/>
          <path d="M8 52 H184" fill="none" stroke="#477fc1" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 3"/>
        </svg>
        <b>(d) Ideal match</b>
        <p>p<sub>g</sub> = p<sub>data</sub> and D(x) = 0.5 wherever they have mass.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="gan-legend"><span><i class="distribution-real"></i> p_data</span><span><i class="distribution-fake"></i> p_g</span><span><i class="distribution-d"></i> D(x)</span></div>
  </section>

  <section id="theory-boundary" class="reading-section">
    <h2>What the proof does not say</h2>
    <p>
      The proof is powerful precisely because its assumptions are explicit. It works in an idealized
      non-parametric setting: G and D have enough capacity, the discriminator can be optimized for the current
      generator, and the generator distribution is updated in a controlled way. Actual networks optimize
      <code>θ<sub>g</sub></code> and <code>θ<sub>d</sub></code>, not arbitrary probability densities directly.
    </p>
    <div class="concept-grid">
      <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Distribution space</h3>
        <p><code>C(p<sub>g</sub>)</code> is convex as a function of the whole distribution under the ideal proof. The unique global optimum matches real data.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Parameter space</h3>
        <p><code>C(G<sub>θ</sub>)</code> need not be convex in neural-network parameters. Gradient descent can meet critical points and game dynamics can oscillate.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Finite training</h3>
        <p>Algorithm performs finite D updates—k = 1 in the reported experiments—instead of actually finding the best discriminator on every step.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <h3>Why disjoint distributions are a useful warning</h3>
    <p>
      If the real and generated distributions have non-overlapping
      <button class="anno" data-anno="support">supports</button>, an ideal discriminator can separate them
      perfectly. In such cases JSD takes its maximal value of <code>log 2</code> (using natural logarithms), even
      though the generator could move its samples in many geometrically different directions. Thus a mathematically
      correct optimum does not guarantee a useful local gradient under an idealized, saturated discriminator.
    </p>
    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">Don't overstate the theorem</span>
      <p>
        The paper proves the <em>desired global objective</em> in a distribution-level idealization and gives a
        corresponding convergence proposition. It does not prove that two finite MLPs trained with ordinary
        alternating SGD must converge from every initialization. Moreover, its JSD derivation concerns the
        original minimax game, not the practical non-saturating loss in isolation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="experiments" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Experiments and evaluation</h2>

    <p>
      A generative model can have a theoretically sound objective without producing
      useful samples in practice. The next question is therefore whether adversarial
      training can actually learn meaningful data distributions from real datasets.
    </p>

    <p>
      Evaluating a generative model is more complicated than evaluating a classifier.
      We want generated samples to look realistic, but realism alone is not enough:
      the generator should also capture the diversity of the underlying data rather
      than repeatedly producing a few convincing examples.
    </p>

    <p>
      The evaluation combines three complementary forms of evidence:
      <strong>quantitative density estimation</strong>,
      <strong>visual inspection of generated samples</strong>, and
      <strong>interpolation through the latent space</strong>.
      Together, they provide an initial picture of what the generator has learned,
      although none of them alone can establish complete distribution matching.
    </p>

    <h3>Datasets and experimental setup</h3>

    <p>
      Three image datasets provide progressively more challenging generation tasks.
      Handwritten digits offer a relatively simple starting point, while human faces
      and natural color images require the model to capture more complicated
      visual structures.
    </p>

    <div class="results-table-wrap">
      <table class="results-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Dataset</th>
            <th>What it contains</th>
            <th>What it tests</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <button class="anno" data-anno="mnist">MNIST</button>
            </td>
            <td>
              28 × 28 grayscale handwritten digits, covering ten digit identities.
            </td>
            <td>
              Whether adversarial training can learn recognizable shapes
              and variations in handwriting.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <button class="anno" data-anno="tfd">TFD</button>
            </td>
            <td>
              Grayscale human faces with variations in facial structure
              and expression.
            </td>
            <td>
              Whether the generator can capture more complicated
              visual relationships than handwritten digits.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <button class="anno" data-anno="cifar">CIFAR-10</button>
            </td>
            <td>
              32 × 32 natural color images from ten object categories.
            </td>
            <td>
              Whether the adversarial framework can extend to
              more diverse natural images and different network architectures.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      The generator uses a combination of rectifier linear and sigmoid activations,
      while the discriminator uses Maxout activations with Dropout. Random noise
      is introduced at the generator's input rather than repeatedly injected
      into its hidden layers.
    </p>

    <p>
      The experiments also explore different network designs. Alongside
      fully connected networks, one configuration uses a convolutional
      discriminator and a deconvolutional generator. This is important because
      adversarial training is not tied to a particular neural network architecture:
      it can be applied to different differentiable models.
    </p>

    <h3>How can we evaluate a generative model?</h3>

    <p>
      Classification has a straightforward evaluation metric. Given an input
      and its correct label, we can simply check whether the model's prediction
      is correct.
    </p>

    <p>
      Generation is different. The input is a random noise vector, and there
      is no unique correct image that the generator is supposed to produce.
      Comparing each generated image with a particular training image would
      therefore be misleading.
    </p>

    <p>
      Ideally, we would like to measure how well the generated probability
      distribution matches the real data distribution. One conventional approach
      is to evaluate the likelihood of unseen test examples under the learned
      model.
    </p>

    <p>
      However, GANs are
      <button class="anno" data-anno="implicitModel">implicit generative models</button>.
      They allow us to draw samples through
      <code>z → G(z)</code>, but do not provide a directly computable
      probability density <code>p_g(x)</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
      To address this, we can construct an approximate density from generated
      samples using a
      <button class="anno" data-anno="parzen">Gaussian Parzen window estimator</button>.
    </p>

    <h4>Estimating probability density from generated samples</h4>

    <p>
      First, draw M independent noise vectors and use the trained generator
      to produce M synthetic images:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \hat{x}_i=G(z_i),
          \qquad z_i\sim p_z,
          \qquad i=1,\ldots,M
        `}
      />
    </div>

    <p>
      Next, place a Gaussian distribution around each generated image.
      Combining these Gaussian kernels gives an estimated density:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \hat{p}(x)=
          \frac{1}{M}
          \sum_{i=1}^{M}
          \mathcal{N}
          \left(x;\hat{x}_i,\sigma^2 I\right)
        `}
      />
    </div>

    <p>
      Here, each generated image <code>ẋ_i</code> serves as the center
      of a Gaussian kernel, while <code>σ</code> controls the kernel width.
      The bandwidth determines how broadly probability mass is distributed
      around each generated sample.
    </p>

    <p>
      Intuitively, if a real test image lies close to many generated samples,
      the estimated density may assign it a relatively high probability.
      If it lies far from the generated samples, its estimated probability
      will generally be lower.
    </p>

    <h4>Computing the test-set log-likelihood</h4>

    <p>
      Once the approximate density has been constructed, we evaluate
      unseen real images under that density. For N test samples,
      the average estimated log-likelihood is:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\mathrm{test}}
          =
          \frac{1}{N}
          \sum_{j=1}^{N}
          \log \hat{p}(x_j)
        `}
      />
    </div>

    <p>
      A higher value generally indicates that the fitted density assigns
      greater probability to the held-out data. The Gaussian bandwidth
      is selected using validation data rather than optimized directly
      on the test set.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">An important evaluation limitation</span>
      <p>
        This is not the exact likelihood of the GAN's generated distribution.
        It is the likelihood under a separate density estimator fitted to
        generated samples. Parzen window estimates can have high variance
        and become unreliable in high-dimensional spaces. Consequently,
        these scores are useful for approximate comparisons but should not
        be treated as definitive measurements of generative quality.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>What do the generated images reveal?</h3>

    <p>
      Quantitative evaluation tells us something about the relationship
      between the generated samples and the test distribution, but it does
      not directly show what the generator has learned visually.
      Examining actual generated images provides complementary evidence.
    </p>

    <p>
      The examples below are random samples from trained generators rather
      than manually selected best results. They illustrate how adversarial
      training performs across handwriting, faces, and natural color images.
    </p>

    <figure class="gan-original-figure">
      <img
        src={originalFigure2}
        alt="Generated handwritten digits, grayscale human faces, and natural color images from early GAN experiments. Yellow outlines identify nearest training examples."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />

      <figcaption>
        <strong>Generated images from the original GAN experiments.</strong>
        The four groups contain handwritten digits, human faces, natural
        color images from a fully connected model, and natural color images
        from a convolutional variant. The yellow-outlined examples are the
        closest training images to their neighboring generated samples.
        Source: Goodfellow et al. (2014).
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="gan-figure-index">
      <div class="gan-figure-card">
        <span>Handwritten digits</span>
        <strong>MNIST</strong>
        <p>
          Many generated digits are recognizable, demonstrating that
          adversarial training can learn meaningful variations in
          handwritten shapes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="gan-figure-card">
        <span>Human faces</span>
        <strong>TFD</strong>
        <p>
          The generated faces contain recognizable facial structures,
          although their details remain blurry and imperfect.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="gan-figure-card">
        <span>Natural color images</span>
        <strong>CIFAR-10: fully connected</strong>
        <p>
          The samples are considerably less realistic than the digit
          examples, revealing the difficulty of modeling more complex
          visual distributions.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="gan-figure-card">
        <span>Alternative architecture</span>
        <strong>CIFAR-10: convolutional variant</strong>
        <p>
          This configuration demonstrates that the adversarial objective
          can be used with convolutional and deconvolutional networks,
          rather than only fully connected layers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h4>Does the generator simply memorize its training data?</h4>

    <p>
      A generative model should learn the underlying data distribution,
      not merely reproduce images it has already seen.
      To investigate this possibility, each group above includes
      nearest-neighbor comparisons.
    </p>

    <p>
      For a generated sample, the nearest-neighbor procedure searches
      the training dataset for the most similar existing image.
      By placing that training image next to the generated sample,
      we can inspect whether the generator appears to be copying
      an example directly.
    </p>

    <p>
      The comparison provides suggestive evidence that the network
      has learned to synthesize new samples rather than simply retrieve
      stored images. However, nearest-neighbor visualization is not
      a rigorous memorization test. It cannot rule out every possible
      form of training-data memorization.
    </p>

    <h3>Latent-space interpolation: what has the generator learned?</h3>

    <p>
      The generated images show what the model can produce, but they
      reveal relatively little about the internal structure of its
      learned mapping.
    </p>

    <p>
      A useful way to investigate that structure is
      <button class="anno" data-anno="latentInterpolation">latent-space interpolation</button>.
      Rather than sampling independent noise vectors, we select
      two points in latent space and gradually move between them.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let the two endpoints be <code>z_a</code> and <code>z_b</code>.
      A linear interpolation between them is defined by:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{aligned}
            z(t)&=(1-t)z_a+tz_b\\[6pt]
            x(t)&=G(z(t))\\[6pt]
            &\quad 0\leq t\leq 1
          \end{aligned}
        `}
      />
    </div>

    <p>
      At <code>t = 0</code>, the input equals the first latent vector.
      At <code>t = 1</code>, it equals the second.
      Intermediate values produce points along the straight line
      connecting them in latent space.
    </p>

    <div
      class="gan-interpolation"
      aria-label="Five successive positions along a linear interpolation between two latent vectors"
    >
      {[
        { t: "t = 0", desc: "z_a" },
        { t: "t = 0.25", desc: "intermediate" },
        { t: "t = 0.5", desc: "midpoint" },
        { t: "t = 0.75", desc: "intermediate" },
        { t: "t = 1", desc: "z_b" }
      ].map((step) => (
        <div class="gan-interpolation-step">
          <strong>{step.t}</strong>
          <div class="gan-interpolation-dot"></div>
          <small>{step.desc}</small>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p>
      We then pass each interpolated vector through the same generator.
      If the learned mapping has meaningful continuous structure,
      nearby latent vectors may produce related images with gradual
      changes in appearance.
    </p>

    <figure class="gan-original-figure gan-original-interpolation">
      <img
        src={originalFigure3}
        alt="Sequences of handwritten digits changing progressively as the input moves along linear paths through the generator's latent space."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />

      <figcaption>
        <strong>Handwritten digits generated along latent-space paths.</strong>
        The shapes change gradually as the input moves between latent
        vectors, suggesting that the generator has learned a continuous
        mapping from noise to recognizable digit structures.
        Source: Goodfellow et al. (2014).
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      These transitions are interesting because the generator is not
      explicitly trained to produce smooth sequences of digits.
      It learns from independent noise samples and discriminator feedback,
      yet intermediate latent points can still produce meaningful images.
    </p>

    <p>
      This provides evidence that the learned mapping contains some
      continuous structure. However, smooth interpolation alone does
      not establish that the generator covers the entire real data
      distribution, has learned disentangled representations, or
      is completely free from memorization.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">What the experiments establish</span>
      <p>
        Adversarial training can produce recognizable synthetic images,
        operate across multiple datasets and neural network architectures,
        and learn latent mappings with meaningful local continuity.
        Approximate density estimates also show competitive performance
        against several earlier generative approaches. These findings
        establish the feasibility of GAN training, while leaving open
        harder questions about sample diversity, quantitative evaluation,
        and training stability.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="results" class="reading-section">
    <h2>What the results actually show</h2>
    <p>
      The paper reports estimated test log-likelihoods under the Parzen-window evaluation
      procedure, with reported uncertainty. Larger numbers are better <em>for this particular estimator</em>.
    </p>
    <div class="results-table-wrap">
      <table class="results-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>MNIST</th><th>TFD</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {tableOne.map((row) => (
            <tr><td>{row.model}</td><td>{row.mnist}</td><td>{row.tfd}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      GAN reports <strong>225 ± 2 on MNIST</strong>, the highest listed MNIST result, and
      <strong>2057 ± 26 on TFD</strong>, below Stacked CAE's reported 2110 ± 50 but above the other two
      listed TFD means. The table alone does not establish statistically decisive superiority on every dataset,
      especially given the estimator's acknowledged limitations.
    </p>
    <div class="comparison-grid">
      <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>What is supported</h3>
        <p>
          Adversarial training can produce recognizable samples and is
          competitive with several contemporary generative-model baselines.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>What is not yet supported</h3>
        <p>
          Universally better image quality, exhaustive mode coverage,
          robust convergence, or consistently higher likelihood
          across different domains.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">Why the result mattered in 2014</span>
      <p>
        The headline achievement was not that these early images looked
        photorealistic. Many are visibly blurry. The achievement was a
        direct and trainable <em>new approach</em> to deep generative
        modeling: a generator learns from a second neural network rather
        than an explicitly evaluated model density or sampling-time
        Markov chain. The experimental evidence was enough to establish
        that the method was worth pursuing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="limitations" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Advantages and limitations</h2>
    <p>
      The same modeling decision that gives GANs their strengths, defining a
      generator by a differentiable sample map rather than a tractable density, also introduces important
      limitations. Think of the design as a change in <em>which computational difficulty is being paid for</em>.
    </p>
    <div class="results-table-wrap">
      <table class="results-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Design choice</th><th>What GAN gains</th><th>What becomes difficult</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Implicit distribution via G(z)</td><td>Fast direct sampling, without repeated Markov transitions.</td><td>No explicit p<sub>g</sub>(x) for likelihood evaluation.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Train D with binary classification</td><td>Leverages mature backpropagation-based discriminative training.</td><td>Requires careful coordination between two changing optimizers.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Use D's gradient to update G</td><td>Does not require paired target images or posterior latent inference.</td><td>Bad or saturated D gradients can make generator updates ineffective.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Flexible differentiable networks</td><td>Does not prescribe a unique network family.</td><td>Finite networks may optimize poorly and miss important data modes.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>1. Why probability evaluation is not free</h3>
    <p>
      Once we define G only as a direct map from noise to samples, we no longer automatically know
      <code>p<sub>g</sub>(x)</code>. This is why the paper needed a fitted Parzen estimator for its quantitative
      comparison. Sampling quickly and assigning accurate likelihoods are different capabilities.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Why generator–discriminator synchronization matters</h3>
    <p>
      If G is updated too aggressively while D is not kept current, it can learn an overly narrow way to fool
      the existing judge. Different z values may map to essentially the same x. In later terminology this is
      <button class="anno" data-anno="modeCollapse">mode collapse</button>; the original paper describes a
      related "Helvetica scenario" in which too many latent inputs map to one output.
    </p>
    <div class="gan-comparison" aria-label="Diverse generation versus collapsed generation">
      <div class="gan-comparison-panel new">
        <span class="gan-step-tag">DIVERSE GENERATOR</span>
        <strong>z₁ → A &nbsp; z₂ → B &nbsp; z₃ → C</strong>
        <p>Different latent inputs cover different important types of real samples.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gan-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">vs</div>
      <div class="gan-comparison-panel old">
        <span class="gan-step-tag">COLLAPSED GENERATOR</span>
        <strong>z₁ → A &nbsp; z₂ → A &nbsp; z₃ → A</strong>
        <p>Images may look convincing individually, yet distributional diversity is missing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>3. The proof and the production system are not the same thing</h3>
    <p>
      The global-optimality proof assumes enormous model capacity and an optimal discriminator. Real networks
      have finite capacity, stochastic updates, non-convex parameterizations, and sometimes incompatible training
      speeds. Therefore <code>D(G(z)) ≈ 0.5</code> on a finite batch is not a guarantee that
      <code>p<sub>g</sub> = p<sub>data</sub></code> in the real world. It might simply mean that the current D
      is weak, undertrained, or blind to missing modes.
    </p>

    <h3>4. A qualified statistical advantage</h3>
    <p>
      The authors suggest that updating G through discriminator gradients, instead of copying training examples
      directly into the generator through a reconstruction objective, might bring a statistical advantage. They also
      point out that GANs can represent sharp distributions without requiring an MCMC sampler to mix between modes.
      These are important hypotheses and design properties, but the original experiment section does not establish
      that GANs never memorize or always maintain good mode coverage.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="future" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Conclusions and future directions</h2>

    <h3>1. Conditional generation</h3>
    <p>
      The paper proposes feeding a condition c into both G and D to model
      <button class="anno" data-anno="cgan">conditional generation</button>. For example, the condition might be
      a requested digit identity. Different noise vectors could then generate different handwritten 7s while
      keeping the requested class fixed.
    </p>
    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula gan-equation">
      <MathFormula
        formula={String.raw`
          \begin{gathered}
            x=G(z,c),\qquad D(x,c)\\[8pt]
            \text{Target distribution: }p(x\mid c)
          \end{gathered}
        `}
      />
    </div>

    <h3>2. Learn inverse inference when it is useful</h3>
    <p>
      Original GAN sampling goes from z to x, with no required encoder. But one can train an auxiliary inference
      network that predicts z given x. The authors note that this network can even be learned after training a
      fixed generator. That extends what the model can do without changing the original direct-sampling idea.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Learn missing variables conditioned on observed variables</h3>
    <p>
      The paper also suggests learning families of conditionals of the form
      <code>p(x<sub>S</sub> | x<sub>¬S</sub>)</code>, with shared parameters. In image terms, S can be the
      unknown pixels and the complement the observed context. The intended extension is broader than digit-class
      conditioning; it includes generating unknown components from known components.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Reuse discriminator features for semi-supervised tasks</h3>
    <p>
      A discriminator learns useful representations while trying to tell real from generated data. Could those
      representations help a classifier when labeled examples are scarce? The authors propose using discriminator
      or learned-inference features for <button class="anno" data-anno="semiSupervised">semi-supervised learning</button>.
    </p>

    <h3>5. Coordinate the game more efficiently</h3>
    <p>
      Finally, the authors propose better G/D coordination and better noise-sampling distributions as possible
      efficiency improvements. This is directly linked to the limitation identified previously: a learning
      problem involving two adaptive networks has to manage the speed and quality of both.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="takeaways" class="reading-section final-takeaways">
    <h2>Takeaways</h2>
    <ol>
      {takeaways.map((item) => (
        <li>{item}</li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>
